Your next smartphone will be able to handle two operating systems
In the future, U.S. Americans are counting on a mobile phone on which the user will switch between different operating systems at the touch of a single button.
Revealed plans sound vacuous.
All you have to do is press one key and you'll be transported to Android in a few seconds.
Exactly this option is aimed at the U.S. company VMware, which focuses mainly on the development of virtualization software for computers.
We will allow you to perform two user profiles on your phone at the same time.
You can switch between them, you can divide them into home and work.
Both of them will work at the same time at the same time, Sristiva Krishnamurti from VMware hinted in an interview with Computerworld magazine.
It was unveiled in November of last year and a few days ago it was presented for the for the first time.
It will be on the market in 2012.
Virtualization of smart phones is no fancy.
VMware has already successfully demonstrated a test sample of a smartphone with two operating systems.
It was a modified Nokia N800 with a 128-MB RAM, on which the aforementioned Windows Mobile and Android systems ran side by side at the same time.
The development of the new technology for mobile phones is in full swing.
Now VMware is working with European and U.S. operators to test smartphone virtualization, and devices are expected to reach customers during 2012.
