Hyundai has complied with the trade union. It will abolish all over-hacks
The management of Nošovice automaker Hyundai agreed with the unions to abolish all over-times.
Precisely due to frequently ordered overtime, the unions declared a strike emergency on Monday.
This was confirmed by Hyundai spokeswoman Petr Vaněk.
However, the management wants to agree with the trade unionists on one more shift this year.
We can't give up on our production plan, it's crucial, that's why voluntary overtime was proposed on Saturday, December 19, with two shifts being made, Vaněk said.
Sedmička.cz reported that the company of employees who work the Saturday shift offered a bonus of CZK 400.
People will get it retrospectively and for a working Saturday, November 28.
According to Vaňek, according to Vaňek, the company has decided to change the ways of motivating employees for the proposed overtime.
They can either pay an over-hackling surcharge or they can take a compensatory leave on December 28 and thus extend the Christmas holidays by a day, Vaněk said.
In addition, all Saturday shift employees will receive paid fares and lunch.
Due to the fact that over-times were the most urgent and pressing point in terms of comments and complaints of trade unionists, as well as the protesting employees who spontaneously halted production last week, the management yesterday afternoon opted to cancel all over-times for the month of December with immediate effect, Vaněk said.
Until the last week, employees of the automaker have been forced to work two-hour overtime virtually every day after an eight-hour shift.
After the signing of the agreement, the unions will call off the strike readiness
Negotiations between the management of the automaker and trade unionists will take place on Friday morning.
Petr Kuchař, the head of the union, said on Wednesday that if they agreed to comply with the demands and sign a joint document, they are ready to cancel the strike readiness.
Situation in the automaker escalated last week on Wednesday, when about 400 employees spontaneously went on strike due to constant overhaul.
They also protested against poor pay conditions and alleged bullying.
The management contradicted the fact that overshoeves must be done by employees in order to meet the requirements for the supply of cars.
Trade unionists also demanded that no sanctuaries be sanctually penalized workers who interrupted production last Wednesday without sanctuary.
Over-temperattauished by unions are asking the company to ease over-temperature work to a minimum.
In addition, they want to pay a reward of 5000 kroner for it this year.
Nošovice automaker Hyundai now employs 2000 people.
Serial production was launched last November.
It has produced about 80,000 cars by September, its current production capacity is 200,000 automobiles per annum.
Czech Discovery: A Substance That Occupies Even ‘Rated’ HIV Viruses
A team of Czech and German scientists is testing new compounds that are able to slow the spread of the HIV virus in an organism.
The main advantage of an improved substance is that it also occupies viruses, which have already acquired immunity against drugs.
In some cases, it outweighs that the compound does not act as strongly as certain already available drugs.
Illest AIDS cannot yet be curtailed.
However, it can significantly prosely prolong the life of a mixture of drugs, which prevent the proliferation of the HIV virus in the body.
However, their use is accompanied by a number of side effects.
Uncommittal suppression of viral proliferation also results in the development of so-called resistant viruses, against which the drugs are already ineffective.
The work of experts from three institutes of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Academy of the Czech Academy of the Czech Republic, from UŠCHT Prague and the University of Heidelberg opens a way to cope with the resurgence of the virus.
They have shown that substances called metalocarborans act on the protein responsible for multiprination of the HIV virus.
Metaloscarborans are compounds of boin, hydrogen, carbon and cobalt.
These compounds block the spread of the virus other than all the drugs used today, and therefore they can overestimate the problem of resurgence.
In their work, the scientists come up with new ‘enhanced’ compounds prepared based on the knowledge of the molecular mechanism of their attachment to a viral protein.
Metalocarborans have a unique three-dimensional structure: two multi-walled cages made up of boron, hydrogen and carbon atoms are intertwined with a metal nucleus, in this case cobalt with cobalt.
Treacherous and resilient virus
HIV prosthease is an HIV protein that is necessary for the life cycle of the virus.
Without an HIV protease due to the cleavage of HIV, there would be no ripe infectious viral particle.
When we halt HIV protease, we also halt the spread of the virus in the patient's body, the scientists explain in a press release published by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic.
Published in Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, scientists describe a series of compounds in which two cage pairs (see table) are intertwined with a short organic chain, which continues to be systematically altered.
Slowly weaker but more reliable
The effect of this series of substances against HIV prostease has been tested in a test tube as well as its resilient (resistant) variants obtained from patients infected with HIV.
The effect of metalco-carborans on a common variant of the enzyme is not as strong as with clinically used drugs, but it does not lose its effect due to resistant variants against which used drugs are often ineffective.
The unique mechanism of action and their other properties, such as biological and chemical stability, low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical modifications from metalcocarborans, make interesting compounds for further research aimed at the design of effective anti-HIV drugs, says Pavlína Řezáčová, Head of Laboratory of Structural Biology of the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry and the Institute of Molecular Genetics of the Academy of Sciences.
Quarrel over budget. ODS has embarked on Fischer, he is defending himself
