Vaccination against pneumococci will be unaccompanied
Vaccination against pneumococci has been devastated since January.
It's supposed to be the first for families to be free and optional at the same time.
However, there are already voices that mandatory vaccination would be more effective.
Preparions to introduce free pneumococci vaccination are culminated.
Pharmaceutical institute already sets the maximum prices of vaccines paid for by health insurers.
Experts are already clear about the vaccination procedure.
New Vaccination
Vaccines free for August children
As of January, children who were born on August 2 last year and later can be vaccinated against pneumococci free of charge.
The condition is that they haven't received a single dose of vaccinated yet.
Those who started vaccinating last year or earlier have to pay for other revaccinations.
The first dose of vaccination must be administered between the 3rd and 5 months of age of the child.
Last year, a quarter of families voluntarily vaccinated against pneumococcal patients were vaccinated.
Parents paid 1600 kroner for one benefit or more.
When the youngest children vaccinated, four doses are needed.
Overall, experts estimate that health insurers spend 300 to 450 thousand crowns a year for vaccination doses.
However, it will save on the treatment that children without vaccination would need.
So far, the state spent about 500 thousand a.m. a year for all blanket vaccination of children.
Paid vaccination has been introduced by the so-called Janota package.
Pneumococcus causes inflammation of the middle ear, as well as serious infections including inflammation of the cerebral membranes and blood poisoning.
Up to 28 children under the age of 10 will die of pneumococcal infections in our country in a year, according to an inquisitory.
In Addition to the Czech Republic, they introduced reimbursed blanket vaccination of children against pneumococci in about 40 countries.
Will Vaccination Responsible?
At the same time, there are opinions that this vaccination could be mandatory in the future.
From January it will be covered by insurance companies and non-obligatory.
Rudolf Kalovský, the head of the Nahlas civic association Nahlas, hinted, for example, that the mandatory vaccination would be better.
His association has long promoted pneumococcal vaccines.
Experts at the international conference, which our association participated, were not completely satisfied with the fact that we have a voluntary vaccination.
It isn't exactly ideal.
On the other hand, we are glad that at least this has been enforced.
I think it's a path that we have to start now, Kalovsky said.
However, the Ministry of Health does not want to change the pneumococcal vaccination procedure.
At this time, we are not considering it at all, his spokeswoman Vlastimil Sršeň said.
Voluntary Vacc Vaccination Has Disadvantages, Physicians Say
The chairwoman of the Professional Society of Practitionary Practition Physicians Hana Cabrnochová recognizes that voluntary vaccination has its drawbacks.
If all children were vaccinated, it might be possible to reduce the number of revaccination "In a situation where this vaccine costs such a high amount, we fairly comprehensibly the political decision that the vaccination was kick-started as voluntary," Cabrnochová said.
Experts await that more than 80 per cent of fresh parents vaccinated against pneumocococcus from next year will be vaccinated.
Vaccination against pneumococci is also groundbreaking in the fact that health insurers, not by the state, as the first widespread vaccination of children, is covered by health insurers.
Pojišt'ovnas should spend money on prevention, thus saving for further treatment, Cabrnochová explains.
Physicians will have to buy substances themselves
However, this is related to the concerns of general pediatricians, which are related to the purchase of vaccines.
According to the original instance, the vaccines should be purchased by insurance companies, and doctors would receive them in a similar way as the state supplies them with substances for other vaccines.
However, some deputies were concerned that insurance companies could "take money out of health insurance".
Therefore, the doctors will have to buy substances and wait for the insurance companies to reimburse them.
According to estimates, individual practical pediatric doctors will invest 40 thousand a month in vaccines, which is a significant sum.
It won't be easy at all, Cabrnoch says.
