It is necessary to deprive all the terrorists there, he thinks.
President Obama, inheriting the two wars in Afghanistan and Iraq from his predecessorGeorge W. Bush, on Thursday in Oslo justified the deployment of forces and used a speech to elucidate his decision to escrow another 30,000 troops into Afghanistan nine days before.
Another Obama's adversary, Newt Gingrich, testified that the president did a "very good job" in Oslo.
I think that the speech was really very good, congratulating the former Speaker of the Chamber of Deputies, who especially appreciated the president's mention of "existence of evil in the world".
In a way, I considered this speech quite historic, Gingrich said during an interview on "The Takeaway".
Former MP, whom some consider a possible Republican nominee in the 2012 presidential election, were thrilled that he sees an "left-wing president" to remind the members of the Nobel Prize committee that they themselves wouldn't be unmarried and couldn't hand over the Peace Prize if there hadn't the use of forces.
According to Michael Gerson, ex-uperception author George W. W. W. W. Bush, now the editor of the Washington Post, Obama delivered a "very American speech."
Not only did he present himself as a "world-citizen" as always, but he "speaking as an U.S. president" and reminded Europeans "that they are living in a safe bubble they didn't create themselves and that they didn't do too much to preserve it," Genson assessed.
Few distasphists from the right include the thresher of the Bush Era, former U.N. ambassador John Bolton, who described the speech "unimagically, pompously and without inspiration," as stated on the National Review Online website.
To the left of the president, Democratic representative Dennis Kucinich is horrified at the sight of Barack Obama justifying the use of forces.
“If we wrap the war in the packaging of justice, we can easily justify the massacre of innocent people,” he warned.
However, the mid-left-left press holds a commendable opinion against the president.
