The Turkish judiciary dissolutely dissolutes for the Kurdish side and this decision entails grievous repercussions.
The Turkish Constitutional Court on Friday ruled abolition of the main pro-Kurdish party in the country. This decision immediately provoked protest demonstrations in the Kurdish community in the south-eastern country and Istanbul.
This decision can greatly complicate the task of the government, which is trying to give a friendly hand to the Kurdish community, thus trying to put an end to 25 years of the conflict.
The Democrat Party (DTP) was dismantled because it became "an nest of activities threatening the independence of the state and its indivisible unity," Supreme Court President Hasim Kilic told reporters.
The decision was taken unanimatelously by 11 judges, when it was necessary to dissolate the party by a majority-seven-vote, Kiliç, adding that 37 party cadres, including its president Ahmet Türk, and Aysela Tuglukova's deputies were banned from participating in political life for a period of five years.
At the same time, the judiciary announced an abolition of parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Mr Türk and Mrs Tugluková and the confisciation of the party's assets by the Ministry of Finance.
The DTP party has 21 seats in Parliament (out 500).
The leadership of the party has already pointed out before the verdict that MEPs would prefer to leave Parliament rather than act as non-participation deputies.
The decision is a follow-up to the trial launched in 2007 by the court's bailout prosecutor Abdurrahman Yalcinkay, who accuse DTP of obedience vis-à-vis the "Commits" of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK), which Ankara and many countries consider a terrorist organization.
Many Observers Convince the DTP is a legal political showcase for PKK rebels.
The party reassigns that it has no "basic connection" with the PKK, but it refuses to designate it as a terrorist organization and calls on the government to negotiate with it.
The verdict of the court comes at a time when the government lends a friendly hand to the Kurdish community and offers a number of measures to strengthen their rights and tries to limit the support of the Kurds against the PKK to end the conflict.
The DTP leadership confirmed that the dismantling of the party could result in resumption of tensions in southeastern Anatolia, where in recent weeks many demonstrations have erected against the conditions under which PKK leader Abdullah Öcalan is deprivated into clashes with the police.
Since announcing the justice decision, thousands of demonstrators have assembled in front of DTP headquarters in Diyarbarkir, a large city in the south-eastern inhabited mostly by Kurds.
The police used lacrimal grenades and water cannons, the demonstrators in turn threw Molotov's cocktails or shot stones out of sausages.
About a hundred people also demonstrated in Istanbul.
Speaking the verdict, Mr Türk said the justice decision would only "detlop the desperdence."
Turkey can not resolve this issue (Kurdes) by banning the party, he said in the press.
The government, formed by a party arising out of the Islamist movement, also condemned the decision.
In principle, we are against the interference of the parties.
It doesn't solve anything, Energy Minister Taner Yildiz said.
This decision is an utter sniplustration of the democratic proposals launched by the government, political scientist Ahmet Insel commented.
Of course, we can expect a reaction from the PKK side, he added.
On Thursday PKK claimed responsibility for one of the deadliest attacks in the north of Turkey, which killed seven soldiers three days earlier.
