Cradle of Dinosaurs
Tawa hallae looked like many other carnivorous dinosaurs: An animal two meters long and 1.50-metre-createn was running on two thick hind limbs, had an old skull with sharp, curved teeth, short forelegs with sickle-shaped claws and a long tail.
If this species lived a few million years later, there wouldn't be anything extraordinary.
Sterling Nesbitt of the University of Texas in Austin and colleagues in Thursday's edition of Science write that Tawa hallae was one of the first dinosaurs with these bodily traits.
The animal lived about 215 million years ago in today's New Mexico, which was at that time in the equatorial drought area of the supercontinent Pangea.
Tawa opens a new window to the earlier evolution of the dinosaurs, Nesbitt says.
It shows us a lot about kinship relationships, extensions and signs of early dinosaurs.
In 2006, Nesbitt and other paleontologists with excavations at Ghost Ranch in the north of New Meexico.
Sometimes in one day we have found a dozen dinosarous bones, it is unparalleled for deposits from the Triassic, reports Randall Irmis from the Museum of Natural History in Utah.
Some of the Bones were quickly identified by scientists as a new species.
During exaggerations they collected the remains from a total of five individuals.
Resumption confirms theories about the early history of Dinosaurs.
The name "Tawa" comes from the sun god Hopi.
For Sterling Nesbitt, it is an unusual finding: "The fossils of dinosaurs from the triassium are very rare, most of the time they find only individual fragments."
Other Dinosaur experts don't share that enthusiasm without reservation.
This finding isn’t particularly sensative, but it confirms some theories about the early history of dinosaurs, says Martin Sander from the University of Bonn.
According to the current scientific opinion, dinosaurs appeared for the first time about 230 million years ago.
A lot of the oldest fossils are not complete, which is why predniosaurus assignment is usually questionable.
Experts are debating, for example, when lizards were divided into the three most important developmental lines: carnivorous throodines, which later belonged Tyranosaurus rex, Velociraptor and also the birds, "long-bloothes" sauropods and their relatives, and herbivores Ornithischia (chaeggings), which included, for example, the species Tricera.
Tawa hallae is, after analysing Nesbitt and his colleagues, an original terrapod.
It exhibits an interesting combination of primitive and advanced traits, Nesbitt says.
Researchers have found, for example, testimonial evidence of aerial vacacies, which even descendants of teropods have birds.
Thanks to aerial vacaceacles, breathing of birds is more effective than mammals.
At Tawa hallae, the scalp's bones were apparently partially filled with air.
Tawa is the eldest and most primitive theropod with air sacs, says Randall Irmis.
Since the relative line of huge sauropods later also had air sacs and slight bones, this useful innovation already probably formed the common anterior of both groups.
At least three migration waves in North America
Knowing the most primitive dinosaurs so far lived in South America, scientists deduced that lizard lizards appeared there for the first time.
According to this, three developmental lines soon split and spread from South America to all continents, which at that time as a supercontinent Pangea formed a monolithic land mass.
The fact that the team, along with Tawa hallae, found the remnants of two other, not particularly closely related theropods.
The three can't come out of one common, mixed an ancestor, scientists argue.
We believe there have been at least three migratory waves in North America, says Alan Turner of the American Museum of Natural History in New York, collaborator of a scientific study.
Controversies, of course there are also contradictions.
One even older species, Silesaurus, which belongs to the group of heaviest dinosaurs, has lived in today's Poland, says Bonn's paleontologist Martin Sander.
