Dreams of altered state of consciousness can occur in a variety of forms, at best we call them visions, at worst delusion.
Although the initiator and effect may be different, the vision of Hildegard von Bingen, the drug experiences of the prophet New-Age Carlos Castaneda, shamanic states of bliss, psychoses and hallucinations derive from similar processes can derive from similar processes.
Hallucinations or visions arise if the balance of neuronal transmitters of the brain is derailed - primarily dopamine, noradrenaline, serotonin and acetylcholine - and the interactions between different parts of the brain are disturbed.
Comprehensive hallucinations and delusional thoughts occur very often during schizophrenic psychosis.
Gravity over it doesn't have the power
Continuity between delusion and the dream occupied philosophers and doctors from antiquity.
Aristotle understood hallucinations as a form of waiver-hair, Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychology, stated in 1861 "a great reluctance to ramaculately with dream states", for Sigmund Freud there was even "psychosis, with all nonsense, delusion, deceiving the senses".
It's autumn 2001, Henning T. turns into a co-comic character.
His body is two-dimensional and gaudy-creatable.
Gravity over it has no power, hovers at the ceiling of the room.
He's feeling a kick.
Behind it winged devilish figures with blood-red grimaces, polluting teeth.
They are chasing him, kicking heavy, fiery boots into him, throwing it against the walls, they feel the pain all over his body.
