Henningu T is 40 years old, he experienced six acute psychoses, the last 2 years ago, the first almost 20 years ago.
LSD has been triggered.
His diagnoses is schizoaffective paranoic psychosis, in addition, he is manic-depressant, takes drugs against depressants, mannia, schizophrenia.
He's learned to live with his illness, he's been married for three years.
In psychology I experienced the worst fears and the deepest doubts, he says.
phericles are a terrible reality
Approximately one per cent of people get sick at least once in a lifetime schizophrenia, in psychiatric clinics it is the most commonly treated disease after depression.
In acute schizophrenic psychosis, those affected usually can no longer distinguish the internal and outer world, they get into an overexcipiated state that can lead to hallucinations, great-grandmother, relationship illusions or the illusion of persecution.
Particularly enscrugnized is the hearing of votes - votes that command, comment on or conduct dialogues.
Optical hallucinations aren’t uncommon.
Disanner is incapacitable to distinguish the importance from unimproteriotable, the brain can not filter out disturbing signals, consciousness is overwhelmed by impressions.
Just as a dream, the psychosis opens a flood of thoughts and fantasies that come from the deepest layers of consciousness.
Perceiving reality in psychology is concentrated on the subject, says Professor Thomas Fuchs, Chief Phnary of the General Psychiatry Clinic at Heidelberg.
Schizophrenic is located in the center of the action, everything has the meaning directed at him.
At the same time, it gets into a passive role, it happens, as in a dream, overwhelmed with events and cannot actively form them.
Schizophrenic therefore often feels threatened, controlled and manipulated.
Comparing with reality is usually not possible for them, phantoms are a terrible reality, waiver is excluded.
Once in a lifetime to experience hallucination
Other than in a dream, Fuchss says, "remains the sensuous and spatial structure of the reality that a person perceives in psychology is fundamentally preserved."
Delusional ideas and hallucinations are based on the reality, alienation of experience occurs in a real framework.
Scientists are still in front of a riddle why exactly our brain is doing such phemes.
Exemptional states in every person can result in a short-stamous loss relationship to reality, Fuchs says.
However, not everyone develops into psychosis.
There appears to be a genetic disposition to schizophrenia, but studies in the twins have shown that genes are not the only determining factor.
Scientists assume that the affected people have minimal brain damage.
We believe that at an early stage of development there will be damage to the neuronal maturation of the brain, such as viral diseases of the mother during pregnancies, Fuchs explains.
But it remains a ridicum of a disparagement of the neuronal system, which has not manifested itself for many years.
Surroundings also play a role - stress or traumatic experiences can initiate psychology if there is a disposition.
Hallucinogenic drugs can also trigger psychosis because they interfere with the transmission system.
When our brain boils
Crazy is that psychotronics suffer their hallucinations, and to get rid of them are swallowing expensive medicamas, others give money for drugs to get them, says psychology professor Erich Kasten of Lübeck.
Whether mirages are perceived as pleasant or annoying, it is often just a matter of stance.
Kasten has been collecting reports of hallucinations and delusions for ten years, last year he interpreted his results in the book "Die irreale Welt in unserem Kopf".
Hallucinations belong to the typical accompanying phenomena of psychoses, but also surprisingly many other diseases.
In Addition to psychological disorders such as boundary personality or depression, often diseases such as migraine, epilepsy, scattered sclerosis or Alzheimer's or cerebral trauma.
Surprisingly hallucinates under certain conditions the very brain of a healthy person.
Hallucination, according to Kasten, consists of content of thoughts that are normally reaspersisted.
Occurhenticated when parts of the brain in which sensory perception is processed are overly active or little resuppressed; if our brain is cooked, or when stimulation is lacking.
Droids can trigger this condition in the same way as cerebral obscenities or psychosis.
But also withdrawal of stimuli, stress, worries with love or other other exceptional states.
In newer studies, up to a third of respondents reported having experienced hallucinations at least once in their lives.
In the summer of 2005, Sarah K. is 16, living in a small town in Mecklburesk-Hilarious Pomerania and goes to the local grammar school.
A perfectly normal girl, in fact.
If there hadn't been those shapes and voices.
It can happen during breaks that she will be approached by a colloquial with long, dark hair.
When he responds, classmates look at her with a surprise - no one can't see and can't hear this boy.
This slant-swoman finds an uptight dreamer, but Sarah K. doesn't dream, she knows that.
He's awake, he hears and sees the boy, just as he hears and sees sitting a girl next to each other in a bench.
It can even feel its presence, for at least a few moments.
It's a pleasant feeling, she likes the boy.
I'm a fool, he asks sometimes.
But he knows this little boy and other turrets aren't real and disappear again.
At night there are rather daunting
Frequencies are often burdened by delusions.
When she sits in the classroom, he can’t watch the lessons many times, because the teacher in her eyes and ears just introduces a new classmate, but he explains to everyone else audibly and viscibly on the table of the mathematical equation.
At night there are rather depressing appearances.
When she waits in his dark room for sleep, which rarely comes and too too late, he cannot often distinguish whether she is awake or if she is already asleep, whether the images he sees, grimace, or a girl with bleeding cuts on her hands and legs are formed in front of her open or closed eyes.
Leave me alone, he's yelling at those figures.
Parents and friends don't say anything about it, they'd think she went crazy.
Autumn 2009. Sarah K. is 20 years old today.
She graduated from graduation last year and lives with her boyfriend.
A year ago hallucinations have ceased.
Ever since she contacted psychologist Kasten on the internet, she's known she hasn't gone crazy.
Delusions are an art of desperation
If someone is socially illuneticed or stimulus is taken away from him, he will most likely have hallucinations, Kasten says.
I don’t think that the brain then doesn’t have a lot of a job and takes care of its entertainment.
As a little little girl, she was afraid of other children as a little girl, she often played on her own.
Her hallucinations began when she learned at 13 that a man she has been told by my father since she thinks she wasn't her own father.
Because in visual and acoustic hallucinations, the same cerebral afflictions, which also process real perceptions and sounds, it is difficult for those affected as Sarah K. to distinguish delusions and reality from each other. "Each experience," Kasten says, "it takes place in the brain, also the reality is depicted there.
Hallucinations are also images that allow the brain to arise, that is why they act so realistically."
Night dreams are also evaluated by Kasten as a form of hallucination.
Hallucination of healthy people does not differ fundamentally from hallucination in acute psychosis.
Compared to schizophrenics, psychologically healthy people are able to identify their delusions as unrealistic.
Kasten therefore advises its patients to evaluate hallucinations as an extraordinary ability.
Boundary state between dream and hallucination
Unfortunately, an imaginary experience is mostly pathologized in our world, says Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, a professor in Heidelberg and chief medical officer of the Psychiatric and psychotherapeutic clinic of Florence-Nightingale Hospital in Düsseldorf.
Psychosis is a severe, torturous disease, but it also brings posibilities, creativity - a delusion is also an art of desperation.
Schmidt-Degenhard examined the so-called oneiroid form of experiences, a special form of altered consciousness for a dream.
Oneiroid states are highly complex, detailed fictitious dreams, which the person concerned considers to be real and does not even re-distingually discriminately from the vigilant state.
One-iroid state of consciousness arises in extreme situations caused by trauma or a disease, such as polyradicular inflammation, who experience motor crippling in full consciousness.
When a person is at risk of the loss of the world and oneself, perhaps a lasting complete incapacity for movement, replaces life-thresponsoring real-life situations in his own, imaginary world that is disconnected away from reality, Schmidt-Degenhard says.
A kind of boundary state between dream and hallucination.
Since it is not possible to cope with a situation in the reality, there will be a change in the worlds, but the real situation will come into appearance.
An intensive form of sensuous support and the transfer of the senses in some unbearable situation, which allows us people their ability to shaping, to lose consciousness in an instant.
It's all real, even later in the memoir
Art historian Peter Cornelius Claussen also conceives his onei-iroid experiences in his book “Herzwechsel” (Herzwechsel) as a life-saving action of the soul.
He was 49 years old when, after a cardiac gallery operation followed by a cardiac arthrophasis, his consciousness embarked on a several-day journey, with his body lying immovably in an intensive care unit.
Claussen immersed himself in the world that he had created, visited foreign times and places: He became an old man confiscated on the bed, in the train sanatorium, the robot guards his health.
It lives in the early Middle Ages among the nobility, which produces narcotic substances from the blood.
On a motorbike, the Greek kidnaps him into his homeland, in a hospital bed he is surfing the company in the Alps and he meets a Korean mobster.
They immerse themselves completely in an imaginary story, everything is real, even later in the memoirs.
These intrinsic paths are fundamentally versidious for Claussen from any dream.
The dream is a different state, it describes its experience.
Opposes to dreams, the memories of those mental pathways are still clearly clear after years and in insignificant details.
Even brighter and more incredulously than a real experience.
The intensity of this explanatory proficiency breaks down the horizon of our other real experiences, says psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard.
After all, they show monoiroid states, psychoses, hallucinations and drug intoxication, how fragile their inner images of external reality are even in a vigilant state.
Every change in the state of consciousness is summarised by the psychologist Kasten, "may lead to a deformation of what we call reality."
Fact, which can also be enriching.
