Zapatero foresees a 'clear-up period' in the automotive industry.
Deputy Prime Minister José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero foresees that the Spanish automobile industry, the state's main export sector, awaits a "cleaning period".
In Prime Minister's economic report for 2009, Zapatero summarised the measures that had been put in place during the year to support the automobile sector.
“While the Competitive Plan for the Automotive Securities, a state of abandonment would await us today, the shut-down of some of our major manufacturing companies,” the Prime Minister said.
"Thanks to this plan and the wishes of individual brands continue to operate in Spain in this highly competitive sector, a certain period of calm for this industry is envisaged in the future, which is our main export sector," he added.
In this regard, it noted that the competitive plan, which forms part of the Integral Automotive Plan (PIA), has identified 800 million for projects of 19 manufacturers and 137 auxiliary firms that mobilized an investment of more than 3 billion euros.
The prime minister explained that this investment is intended for the development of new models, for the modernization of production and existing processes.
Zapatero also emphasized the government's measures to support the requirement and mentioned the 2000E plan, which is applied in cooperation with autonomous areas and car brands.
According to the president's statement, this plan made it possible to reduce by 6% the average emissions of new vehicles that are being sold in the Spanish market, ensuring a "significal increase" of enrollments, namely "40 per cent in November".
