Brazil sets Zelay's exit date
The Brazilian government has set a deadline to remain Manuel Zelay at its embassy in Tegucigapa.
An ousted president must relocate a diplomatic mission no later than January 27, 2010, when his mandate officially expires.
Francisco Catunda, chargé d'affaires of the embassies in the Honduran capital, confirmed to TV Globo that "Mel" knows they have to retire until that date from the embassies where it has been hiding since September 21.
“Acknowledging the fact that on January 27, after the end of his mandate, he has to retire and seek another refuge.”
The station also quoted a former stateswoman who confirmed his intent to leave the embassy before a set deadline: "I intend to leave as soon as possible, logically under the auspices of the Brazilian government", Zelaya said by phone.
Speaking on November 29 (when Porfirio Lobo was elected), Zelaya reassured that he would stay at the Brazilian embassy until January.
However, the same week he attempted to take refuge in Brazil.
The operation eventually crashed because Zelaya rejected the proprietor of political asylum, which the government "de facto" presided over by Roberto Micheletti.
