Playing in the League of Champions from the inception brought 476.4 million Madrid and Barça
The Champions League has become a source of income for these clubs since its incorporation in 1992.
Real Madrid and Barcelona have already made $476.4 million since then, which is the third and fifth-highest-effigy club in the competition.
The document is led by Manchester United.
The document is led by Manchester United.
Winning the UEFA Champions League doesn't just bring fame. It's also one of the main sources of income for big clubs.
To such an extent that the competition has allocated €5.362.5 million among the 105 cooperatives (of which 12 Spanish ones), which took part in this event since the creation of the 1992/1993 tournament, according to UEFA data.
Real Madrid and F. C. Barcelona came out very well from this division.
The white club was the third on the continent with the most money it received for participation, and the only one along with AC Milan, who won the main prize three times.
Catalan's current champison and the last winner of the defunct European Cup in 1992 is the fifth.
The list is hosted by Manchester United and Bayern in Munich, two squads, who additionally accounted the most money for participating in the previous Champions League.
This is despite the fact that she both lost to Barça.
On the other hand, Spain is only the third country, which received the most money through the Champions, even though it won the most titles of all (ffive against four for Italy, three for England and two for Germany).
Distribution
This apparent paradox is explained by a complex procedure for dividing money used by UEFA.
For this financial year, the body chaired by Michel Platini plans to make 1,019 million for the Champions League.
From this UEFA money donates $413.1 million for fixed-to-pay: each of the 20 clubs that took part in a preliminary classification round (including Atlético Madrid) received 2.1 million.
In addition, those who have achieved participation received 3.8 million only for their presence, in addition to an additional €550,000 for each match they participate.
There is also a premium for a win ($800,000) and a draw ($400,000). Eight-finalists will receive three-million each; eight quarter-finalists3,3 million.
